<!--
doc/src/sgml/plisql.sgml
IvorySQL documentation: PL/iSQL overview
-->

<chapter id="plisql">
 <title>PL/iSQL - Oracle-Compatible Procedural Language</title>

 <indexterm zone="plisql">
  <primary>PL/iSQL</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <para>
  PL/iSQL is the Oracle-compatible procedural language for IvorySQL.
  It extends the standard PL/pgSQL with Oracle PL/SQL semantics including
  packages, autonomous transactions, nested subprograms, Oracle exception
  types, and Oracle-compatible control flow syntax.
 </para>

 <sect1 id="plisql-overview">
  <title>Overview</title>
  <para>
   PL/iSQL is a block-structured procedural language that closely follows
   Oracle PL/SQL conventions. It is the default procedural language when
   IvorySQL operates in Oracle-compatible mode (port 1521 by default).
  </para>
  <para>
   Key differences from PL/pgSQL:
  </para>
  <itemizedlist>
   <listitem>
    <para>Package support (CREATE PACKAGE / CREATE PACKAGE BODY)</para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>Autonomous transactions via PRAGMA AUTONOMOUS_TRANSACTION</para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>Nested subprograms (procedures and functions declared within a block)</para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>Oracle exception types (NO_DATA_FOUND, TOO_MANY_ROWS, DUP_VAL_ON_INDEX, etc.)</para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>FOR loop with implicit cursor (SELECT ... loop without explicit cursor declaration)</para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>OUT parameters in procedures</para>
   </listitem>
   <listitem>
    <para>Oracle-compatible assignment and expression syntax</para>
   </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="plisql-structure">
  <title>Block Structure</title>
  <para>
   A PL/iSQL block has the following structure:
  </para>
<programlisting>
[ DECLARE
    declaration_statements ]
BEGIN
    executable_statements
[ EXCEPTION
    exception_handlers ]
END;
</programlisting>
  <para>
   The DECLARE section is optional. The BEGIN...END block is mandatory.
   The EXCEPTION section is optional and handles runtime errors.
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="plisql-packages">
  <title>Package Support</title>
  <para>
   PL/iSQL supports Oracle-style packages through CREATE PACKAGE and
   CREATE PACKAGE BODY statements. A package specification declares
   public types, variables, cursors, functions, and procedures. The
   package body provides implementations.
  </para>
<programlisting>
CREATE PACKAGE emp_pkg AS
    FUNCTION get_salary(emp_id INTEGER) RETURN NUMERIC;
    PROCEDURE raise_salary(emp_id INTEGER, amount NUMERIC);
END emp_pkg;
/

CREATE PACKAGE BODY emp_pkg AS
    FUNCTION get_salary(emp_id INTEGER) RETURN NUMERIC IS
        v_sal NUMERIC;
    BEGIN
        SELECT sal INTO v_sal FROM emp WHERE empno = emp_id;
        RETURN v_sal;
    END;

    PROCEDURE raise_salary(emp_id INTEGER, amount NUMERIC) IS
    BEGIN
        UPDATE emp SET sal = sal + amount WHERE empno = emp_id;
    END;
END emp_pkg;
/
</programlisting>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="plisql-autonomous">
  <title>Autonomous Transactions</title>
  <para>
   PL/iSQL supports autonomous transactions through the
   PRAGMA AUTONOMOUS_TRANSACTION directive. An autonomous transaction
   runs independently of the calling transaction and can commit or
   roll back without affecting the caller.
  </para>
<programlisting>
PROCEDURE log_action(msg TEXT) IS
    PRAGMA AUTONOMOUS_TRANSACTION;
BEGIN
    INSERT INTO audit_log(message) VALUES (msg);
    COMMIT;
END;
</programlisting>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="plisql-exceptions">
  <title>Exception Handling</title>
  <para>
   PL/iSQL provides Oracle-compatible named exceptions:
  </para>
  <informaltable>
   <tgroup cols="2">
    <thead>
     <row><entry>Exception</entry><entry>Description</entry></row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
     <row><entry>NO_DATA_FOUND</entry><entry>SELECT INTO returned no rows</entry></row>
     <row><entry>TOO_MANY_ROWS</entry><entry>SELECT INTO returned more than one row</entry></row>
     <row><entry>DUP_VAL_ON_INDEX</entry><entry>Unique constraint violation</entry></row>
     <row><entry>VALUE_ERROR</entry><entry>Arithmetic, conversion, or size error</entry></row>
     <row><entry>ZERO_DIVIDE</entry><entry>Division by zero</entry></row>
     <row><entry>INVALID_CURSOR</entry><entry>Cursor operation not allowed</entry></row>
    </tbody>
   </tgroup>
  </informaltable>
<programlisting>
BEGIN
    SELECT sal INTO v_sal FROM emp WHERE empno = 9999;
EXCEPTION
    WHEN NO_DATA_FOUND THEN
        DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE(Employee not found);
    WHEN TOO_MANY_ROWS THEN
        DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE(Multiple rows returned);
END;
</programlisting>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="plisql-subprograms">
  <title>Nested Subprograms</title>
  <para>
   PL/iSQL allows declaring procedures and functions within a block
   declaration section. These nested subprograms are local to the
   enclosing block and can reference variables from the outer scope.
  </para>
<programlisting>
DECLARE
    v_total NUMERIC := 0;
    PROCEDURE add_value(n NUMERIC) IS
    BEGIN
        v_total := v_total + n;
    END;
BEGIN
    add_value(10);
    add_value(20);
    DBMS_OUTPUT.PUT_LINE(Total:  || v_total);
END;
</programlisting>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="plisql-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>
  <para>
   PL/iSQL is an IvorySQL extension that provides Oracle PL/SQL
   compatibility. It is not available in standard PostgreSQL. The
   language is automatically available in Oracle-compatible mode.
   For PostgreSQL-native procedural code, use PL/pgSQL.
  </para>
 </sect1>
</chapter>
